# Erdos Problem #99 - loaded-flat-center weak-contact collapse at gap 0.002123

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical contact-minimal full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** certified minimax phase-star atomic gap `g=0.002123`; low-t flat-center multiplicity one; loaded-flat-center singleton gate; flat-center contact-degree trichotomy; global degree-three stress estimate
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The current flat-center analysis leaves loaded centers, unloaded degree-three centers,
and unloaded degree-four centers. Paying the loaded centers by singleton trains gives a
linear unloaded remainder. Previously the degree-four remainder was sent to a hidden
near-contact activation branch. That split is unnecessary.

At an unloaded degree-four flat center, the two contacts on the complementary side of
the almost-straight pentagon angle also have asymptotically vanishing KKT weight. Thus
**every unloaded flat center**, whether degree three or four, contributes actual weak
positive contact edges. After endpoint multiplicity is paid, the entire low-`t`,
small-residual branch has one common source-level degeneracy: linearly many exact
contacts carrying only `o(D^2)` total normalized KKT weight.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad g:=0.002123.
\]

On a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t:=\lim {q-k\over D},\qquad
\delta:=\lim {b-P_F\over D},\qquad
\kappa:=\lim {k\over D},
\]

and assume

\[
R_F=o(D).
\tag{WC.1}
\]

Let `L` be the loaded flat centers and let `U=U_3\sqcup U_4` be the unloaded degree-three
and degree-four flat centers. Then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {|U|\over D}
\ge
U_g(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=2\pi+3s+{3g\over2}-5t+2\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
}
\tag{WC.2}
\]

There is a set `E_{weak}` of distinct positive contact edges satisfying

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {|E_{weak}|\over D}
\ge {1\over2}U_g(t,\delta,\kappa),
}
\tag{WC.3}
\]

and, for the canonical relative-interior normalized KKT stress,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\in E_{weak}}\alpha_e=o(D^2).
}
\tag{WC.4}
\]

Thus the low-`t`, small-residual branch no longer has a degree-three/degree-four escape:
a positive unloaded-center floor automatically forces a linear weak-contact family.

## 1. Updated unloaded-center floor

The `g=0.002123` minimax theorem gives both

\[
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}\ge2s+g
\tag{WC.5}
\]

and, on the strict-rhombus train graph,

\[
\liminf {T_R\over D}\ge2s+g.
\tag{WC.6}
\]

The low-`t` pentagon ledger therefore gives the distinct flat-center floor

\[
\liminf {V_{flat}\over D}
\ge
\pi+2s+g-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{WC.7}
\]

Every loaded flat center contributes two distinct singleton stopped trains, so

\[
2|L|\le S,
\tag{WC.8}
\]

where `S=T-T_R` is the singleton population. The exact terminal ledger and `f<=Q`
give

\[
\limsup {T\over D}\le5t-2\pi-2\delta.
\tag{WC.9}
\]

Subtracting (WC.6),

\[
\limsup {S\over D}
\le5t-2\pi-2\delta-2s-g,
\tag{WC.10}
\]

hence

\[
\limsup {|L|\over D}
\le {5t\over2}-\pi-\delta-s-{g\over2}.
\tag{WC.11}
\]

Since `U=V_flat-L`, (WC.7)--(WC.11) give exactly (WC.2).

## 2. Degree-three centers: the existing weak edge

Let `m in U_3`. Besides the two flat-face contacts `ml,mr`, there is one exterior
contact `mx`. If the large face angle is at least `pi-epsilon`, the existing local KKT
estimate gives

\[
\alpha_{mx}
\le
R(\epsilon)\min\{\alpha_{ml},\alpha_{mr}\},
\qquad
R(\epsilon):={\sin\epsilon\over\sin(\pi/3-\epsilon)}.
\tag{WC.12}
\]

On the diagonal flatness scale `epsilon_D->0`, summing over all degree-three centers and
using the full-support virial identity

\[
\sum_{e\in C}\alpha_e=D^2
\tag{WC.13}
\]

gives the already-proved occurrence bound

\[
\sum_{m\in U_3}\alpha_{e_m}
\le R(\epsilon_D)D^2=o(D^2).
\tag{WC.14}
\]

There is one selected exterior-edge occurrence per center in `U_3`.

## 3. Degree-four centers also force weak exact contacts

Let `m in U_4`. Write the four contact neighbors in cyclic order so that the bounded
pentagon uses the large sector from `l` to `r`, while the complementary arc runs

\[
r,\ x,\ y,\ l.
\]

Let its three complementary angular gaps be

\[
\theta_i={\pi\over3}+\eta_i,
\qquad \eta_i>0,
\]

with

\[
\eta_1+\eta_2+\eta_3=:\eta\le\epsilon.
\tag{WC.15}
\]

Rotate coordinates so the contact direction `mr` has angle zero. Then the other
contact directions have angles

\[
0,
\quad {\pi\over3}+\eta_1,
\quad {2\pi\over3}+\eta_1+\eta_2,
\quad \pi+\eta.
\tag{WC.16}
\]

The center is unloaded, so positive KKT equilibrium is

\[
\alpha_r u_r+\alpha_xu_x+\alpha_yu_y+\alpha_lu_l=0.
\tag{WC.17}
\]

Take the component perpendicular to `u_r`. Because

\[
\sin\left({\pi\over3}+\eta_1\right)
\ge \sin(\pi/3-\epsilon),
\]

and, using `\eta_1+\eta_2<=\eta<=\epsilon`,

\[
\sin\left({2\pi\over3}+\eta_1+\eta_2\right)
\ge \sin(\pi/3-\epsilon),
\]

while

\[
\sin(\pi+\eta)=-\sin\eta,
\]

equation (WC.17) gives

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_x+\alpha_y
\le
R(\epsilon)\alpha_l.
}
\tag{WC.18}
\]

Thus both complementary contacts of every unloaded degree-four flat center are weak in
aggregate, with the same vanishing factor as the degree-three estimate.

Sum (WC.18) over `U_4`. Each selected face edge `ml` can occur for at most its two
endpoints, so

\[
\sum_{m\in U_4}\alpha_{ml}
\le2D^2.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{m\in U_4}(\alpha_{mx}+\alpha_{my})
\le2R(\epsilon_D)D^2=o(D^2).
}
\tag{WC.19}
\]

No lower bound on any individual contact multiplier is used.

## 4. Deduplicate all unloaded weak edges at once

Select one exterior edge occurrence at every center in `U_3` and both complementary
edge occurrences at every center in `U_4`. The total number of selected occurrences is

\[
|U_3|+2|U_4|.
\tag{WC.20}
\]

One undirected contact edge can be selected at most twice, once from each endpoint.
Hence the set `E_weak` of distinct selected edges obeys

\[
|E_{weak}|
\ge {|U_3|+2|U_4|\over2}
\ge {|U_3|+|U_4|\over2}
={|U|\over2}.
\tag{WC.21}
\]

Combining (WC.14) and (WC.19), and noting that deduplication cannot increase a sum of
nonnegative weights,

\[
\sum_{e\in E_{weak}}\alpha_e
\le3R(\epsilon_D)D^2=o(D^2).
\tag{WC.22}
\]

Equations (WC.21)--(WC.22) prove (WC.3)--(WC.4).

## 5. Current endpoint constants

The certified `g=0.002123` Section-171 endpoint is

\[
t_{23}=C_{146}+0.0017327,
\qquad C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+s),
\]

so

\[
t_{23}=1.82124623797944\ldots .
\]

At `delta=kappa=0`, the current flat-center floor is

\[
\boxed{
{V_{flat}\over D}
\ge0.495225672369096\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{WC.23}
\]

The unloaded-center floor is

\[
\boxed{
{|U|\over D}
\ge0.0370770378742350\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{WC.24}
\]

so (WC.3) becomes

\[
\boxed{
{|E_{weak}|\over D}
\ge0.0185385189371175\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{WC.25}
\]

with total normalized KKT weight `o(D^2)`.

Even after discarding the nonnegative `delta,kappa` bonuses, the unloaded floor is
positive throughout

\[
\boxed{
t< {2\pi+3s+3g/2\over5}
=1.82866164555429\ldots .}
\tag{WC.26}
\]

Consequently, excluding a linear weak-contact family of the form (WC.3)--(WC.4) would
by itself raise the proof-only Type-II `q-k` floor from `1.8212462...` to at least
`1.8286616...` on the small-residual branch.

## 6. Frontier effect

The low-`t`, small-residual flat-pentagon endpoint is now reduced to one stress
phenomenon rather than a hidden-contact/weak-contact dichotomy:

```text
>= 0.0185385 D distinct exact positive contacts
with total normalized KKT weight o(D^2).
```

The degree-four hidden near-pairs still exist and remain useful geometric information,
but they are no longer required to state the unavoidable degeneration.

This does not yet contradict relative-interior KKT positivity: a finite positive stress
can assign arbitrarily small weights to a linear contact family. The next closing
lemma must use where these weak contacts sit in the pentagon/train network, or show
that simultaneously weakening them produces a contact-preserving mixed flex or a
source-preserving diameter descent.

No exact new unit contact, unit equilateral triangle, or strict same-cardinality
sub-`D` source is asserted here.

## 7. Scope audit

- The scalar constant `g=0.002123` is the current strongest certified proof-only
  minimax endpoint and inherits its audit lifecycle.
- The loaded singleton payment is unchanged and finite.
- Degree-four estimate (WC.18) uses only unloaded KKT equilibrium and the already-proved
  angular gaps of a flat degree-four center.
- The global weight sum uses the canonical relative-interior full-support
  normalization `sum alpha_e=D^2`.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source and do not enter the flat
  center count.
- The count in (WC.21) is occurrence-safe: one undirected contact edge has only two
  endpoints.

C-II and Level I remain open.
